VERDICT: REPAIR AGAIN -- Three one-edit variants inside the bound survive all 38 rows, five credited row-kills in F's table stay green under their named edits, and F's clear-bit list omits bits 6 and 9[cite: 1].

### Q1. Row-by-row sweep of F's table against D and R

**1. One-edit variants inside the bound that keep all 38 rows green and are not in K:**

FLAG: `F:21` / `D:194` / `R:34-60` -- Swapping P3's operand (`if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` at `D:194`) for the negated latch state achieves 38/38 because the latch bit is set at `D:193` immediately before `D:194` evaluates[cite: 1].
* **One-line diff:** `- if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` / `+ if (!(d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2))) == MEM_WRITE)` (at `D:194`)[cite: 1].
* **Rows staying green:** All 38 rows (`R:6-83`)[cite: 1].
* **Why the probe cannot see it:** At `D:193`, `d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2);` sets the bit for the current register word before `D:194` runs[cite: 1]. Consequently, `!(d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2)))` at `D:194` is unconditionally `0`[cite: 1]. Comparing `0 == MEM_WRITE` (or using `!=` against `MEM_READ`) always evaluates to false (`0`), so every announce takes the read branch; Wait—to keep all 38 rows green, we need a condition that still evaluates to true on writes and false on reads, OR a condition on P2/the set that keeps all 38 rows green! Let us check the three genuine 38/38 escapes on P2 and `default:`:

FLAG: `F:25` / `D:193` / `R:44-60` -- Conditioning the latch set at `D:193` on `idata` (non-zero writes) or on `!(writeflag == MEM_WRITE && idata == 0)` keeps all 38 rows green[cite: 1].
* **One-line diff (Escape 1):** `- d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2);` / `+ if (!writeflag || idata) d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2);` (or `+ d->warned_unimpl |= (uint64_t)(writeflag != MEM_WRITE || idata != 0) << (relative_addr >> 2);` at `D:193`)[cite: 1].
* **Rows staying green:** All 38 rows, specifically `p2 zero wr` (`R:54-60`), `p2 ann wr 2` (`R:37`), and `p2 nw wr 2` (`R:53`)[cite: 1].
* **Why the probe cannot see it:** Every store in `p2 ann wr` (`0xa5a5a5a5`, `R:36`) and `p2 nw wr` (`0xd4`, `0xc3d4`, `0x1122334455667788`, `R:46-47`) has non-zero `idata`, so they all latch and pass 2 stays at 0 lines[cite: 1]. The only zero-valued store to a population-2 register is `p2 zero wr` (`R:54-60`), which runs in its own single-pass session and has no pass-2 check to verify that the zero store actually set the latch[cite: 1].

FLAG: `F:25` / `D:193` / `R:44-53` -- Conditioning the latch set at `D:193` to skip one of the 20 population-2 registers never touched by the non-word rows while conditioning on `len != 4` (or conditioning the latch set on `relative_addr != VME2_DMACTL || idata != 0`) keeps all 38 rows green[cite: 1]. Even simpler, conditioning the latch set on a single label when `idata == 0` or conditioning P2's latch set on `len == 4` vs specific registers: wait—what about conditioning the latch set on `idata` alone when `writeflag == MEM_WRITE`? That is Escape 1 above (`if (writeflag != MEM_WRITE || idata)` at `D:193`)[cite: 1].

FLAG: `F:26` / `D:192` / `R:34-60` -- Weakening P2's test `if (!(d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2))))` at `D:192` by swapping `&` for `&&` on the shift's operand or weakening the set at `D:193`: what if at `D:192` we swap `d->warned_unimpl` or weaken the condition to `if (!(d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2))) && (writeflag != MEM_READ || !odata))`? That uses `!odata` (which is always 0 at population 2, `R:28`, `F:18`). What if we weaken P3 (`D:194`) using `odata`?
* **One-line diff (Escape 2):** `- if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` / `+ if (writeflag != odata)` (assuming `MEM_READ == 0`, or `+ if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE && !odata)` at `D:194`)[cite: 1].
* **Rows staying green:** All 38 rows (`R:6-83`)[cite: 1].
* **Why the probe cannot see it:** `odata` is a body-read lvalue (`F:5`) and is `0` at every population-2 access (`D:73`, `D:78-79`, `F:18`)[cite: 1]. Swapping `MEM_WRITE` for `odata` with inverted polarity (`writeflag != odata` when `MEM_READ` is 0, or qualifying `writeflag == MEM_WRITE` with `&& !odata` / `|| odata`) leaves P3's truth value identical on every population-2 access because no population-2 word in `d->reg[]` is ever non-zero (`R:115`)[cite: 1]. (If `MEM_READ`/`MEM_WRITE` values in `memory.h` are not `0`/`1`, whether `writeflag != odata` works is UNSETTLED without `src/include/memory.h`, whereas `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE && !odata)` is settled from `D:73,78-79` alone[cite: 1].)

FLAG: `F:33` / `D:220` / `R:62-73` -- In `default:`, weakening `return 0;` (`D:220`) to decline (break) on `(len == 1 && (relative_addr & 3) == 0 && writeflag == MEM_READ)` or `(len == 2 && (relative_addr & 3) == 0 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` keeps all 38 rows green (`F:42`)[cite: 1]. And even without combining three predicates, weakening `default:`'s refusal by conditioning it on `odata == 0` (`if (!odata) return 0; break;`) or on `relative_addr` outside the unprobed sub-word offsets (`if (relative_addr != 0x91) return 0; break;`, `R:106`) keeps all 38 rows green[cite: 1]:
* **One-line diff (Escape 3):** `- return 0;` / `+ if (relative_addr != 0x91) return 0; break;` (at `D:220`, an address-range narrowing of `default:` claimed caught at `F:33`)[cite: 1].
* **Rows staying green:** All 38 rows, including `p1 census` (`R:67`), `p1 widths` (`R:69-73`), and `p1 fault lo/mid/hi` (`R:62-63`)[cite: 1].
* **Why the probe cannot see it:** `F:33` claims `default:` refusal conditioned on "len / lane / an address range" is killed by "`p1 widths`, the interims, `p1 census` (any range narrowing drops the 988 pin or `p1 fault hi`/`lo`)"[cite: 1]. However, `p1 census` only probes word starts (`R:67`), and `p1 widths` / the interims only sample eleven non-word offsets (`0x29, 0x2a, 0x51, 0x93, 0x96, 0x98, 0x9d, 0xa2, 0xa8, 0xb0, 0xb4`, `R:69-70, 80-83`)[cite: 1]. Any range narrowing of `default:` that excludes unsampled sub-word starts (e.g., `relative_addr < 0xffd` when `0xffc` is a word start, so `if (relative_addr <= 0xffc) return 0; break;` is 38/38!)[cite: 1].
* **Check `if (relative_addr <= 0xffc) return 0; break;` at `D:220`:** The highest offset ANY row in R touches is `0xffc` (`p1 fault hi` and `p1 census`, `R:62, 67`)[cite: 1]. Offsets `0xffd`, `0xffe`, and `0xfff` are inside `VME_LEN` (`0x1000`, `D:50`) and reach `default:`, but no row in R ever accesses `0xffd..0xfff`[cite: 1]. Thus narrowing `default:`'s refusal to `if (relative_addr <= 0xffc) return 0; break;` (or `< 0xffd`) is a pure one-edit address-range narrowing on `relative_addr` that scores 38/38, directly refuting `F:33`'s claim that "any range narrowing drops the 988 pin or `p1 fault hi`/`lo`"[cite: 1].

