**2. Rows F credits with a kill that DO NOT go red under the edit F names:**

FLAG: `F:21` / `D:193-194` / `R:34, 36` -- F claims swapping P3's operand (`writeflag == MEM_WRITE` at `D:194`) for "the latch state" makes the condition "always true after the set: every announce is write-form, `p2 ann rd`"[cite: 1].
* **One-line diff:** `- if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` / `+ if (!(d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2))))` (swapping P3's operand for "the latch state" P2, or for `d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2))` if negated)[cite: 1].
* **Rows staying green / going red oppositely:**
  1. If swapped literally for P2's latch-state expression `!(d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2)))`, because `D:193` has ALREADY set the bit, the expression is **always false (0)** at `D:194`, not always true[cite: 1]. Every announce is **read-form**, so `p2 ann rd` (`R:34`) **stays green** (and `p2 ann wr` at `R:36` goes red instead)[cite: 1].
  2. Even if swapped for the un-negated field `if (d->warned_unimpl)` (or `if (d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2)))`), the edit is `if (d->warned_unimpl == MEM_WRITE)` under a single-operand swap of `writeflag` (leaving `== MEM_WRITE` intact)[cite: 1]. Since `d->warned_unimpl` is a 64-bit bitmask (`1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2)` where `relative_addr >> 2` is in `1..35` for all 26 population-2 registers, as offset `0` is cased at `D:83`), `d->warned_unimpl` is never `0` or `1` (`MEM_WRITE`), so `d->warned_unimpl == MEM_WRITE` is **always false** after `D:193`! Thus `p2 ann rd` (`R:34`) **stays green** under a literal operand swap of `writeflag` for `d->warned_unimpl`[cite: 1].

FLAG: `F:25` / `D:193` / `R:35, 37` -- F claims conditioning the latch set at `D:193` on `idata` causes "non-zero writes never latch -> `p2 ann wr 2`" (or if conditioned on `if (idata)`, zero writes and reads never latch)[cite: 1].
* **One-line diff:** `- d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2);` / `+ if (idata) d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2);` (at `D:193`, conditioning the set on `idata`)[cite: 1].
* **Rows staying green:** Under `if (idata)` (positive conditioning on `idata`), non-zero writes DO latch (`idata` is `0xa5a5a5a5` in `p2 ann wr`, `R:36`), and in `p2 ann wr` each register is WRITTEN first (setting the latch) and then immediately READ (`R:36`), so in `p2 ann wr 2` (`R:37`) every latch is already set by pass 1's write! Thus `p2 ann wr 2` **stays green** (`0` lines) under `if (idata)` (it is `p2 ann rd` / `p2 ann rd 2` that go red because reads have `idata == 0` and don't latch until the paired write in pass 1, producing 26 write-form lines in `p2 ann rd` pass 1)[cite: 1]. Conversely, under `if (!idata)` (negative conditioning on `idata`, where non-zero writes do not latch), in `p2 ann wr` (`R:36`) each register is WRITTEN first (doesn't latch because `idata != 0`) and **immediately READ in pass 1** (`R:36`: "each WRITTEN then immediately READ")[cite: 1]. That immediate read in pass 1 has `idata == 0`, so the read in pass 1 **announces AND sets the latch**! Because pass 1's paired read sets the latch for all 26 registers, pass 2 (`p2 ann wr 2`, `R:37`) sees all 26 latches already set and prints **0 lines**[cite: 1]!
* **Why `p2 ann wr 2` cannot see `if (!idata)` on the latch set:** `R:36` states that `p2 ann wr` executes "each WRITTEN then immediately READ" in pass 1, and `R:37` (`p2 ann wr 2`) runs "the same pairs again: 0 lines"[cite: 1]. When non-zero writes fail to set the latch (`if (!idata)` at `D:193`), the write in pass 1 announces (write-form) without latching; then the immediate read in pass 1 sees the latch still clear, announces (read-form, failing `p2 ann wr` pass 1), and **sets the latch** because `idata == 0` on a read (`D:73, 75`)[cite: 1]. By the time `p2 ann wr 2` (pass 2) runs, all 26 latches are set, so `p2 ann wr 2` outputs 0 lines and **stays green**[cite: 1]! (`K:16` confirms this exact mechanism for `M_LATCHRDSET` (`if (writeflag == MEM_READ)` on the set): `M_LATCHRDSET` only reds `p2 ann wr` and `p2 nw wr 2`, while **`p2 ann wr 2` stays green**[cite: 1]!)

FLAG: `F:25` / `D:193` / `R:35` -- F claims conditioning the latch set at `D:193` on `odata` is killed by `p2 ann rd 2` (or `p2 ann wr 2`)[cite: 1].
* **One-line diff:** `- d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2);` / `+ if (!odata) d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << (relative_addr >> 2);` (at `D:193`)[cite: 1].
* **Rows staying green:** All 38 rows stay green if conditioned on `!odata` because `odata` is always `0` at population 2 (`D:73, 78-79`, `F:18`); and if conditioned positively on `if (odata)`, then nothing ever latches (equivalent to `M_LATCHNOSET`, `K:15`), which reds 6 rows (`p2 ann rd`, `p2 ann rd 2`, `p2 ann wr`, `p2 ann wr 2`, `p2 nw rd 2`, `p2 nw wr 2`), whereas `if (!odata)` keeps every row green[cite: 1].

FLAG: `F:27` / `D:192-193` / `R:26, 35` -- F claims swapping the latch field `d->warned_unimpl` for `d->reg[...]` "never latches (always-announce) -> `p2 ann rd 2`; a `d->reg[] |=` additionally shows in `p2 rd census` read-backs"[cite: 1].
* **One-line diff:** Swap `d->warned_unimpl` in `D:192-193` for `d->reg[0]` (or `d->reg[relative_addr >> 2]`)[cite: 1].
* **Rows staying green:**
  1. If swapped for `d->reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)]` (or `d->reg[0]`) in **both** `D:192` and `D:193`, `d->reg[...]` is persistent across calls in the same session (`D:53`) and is not overwritten by population-2 accesses (`D:159-160`)[cite: 1]. Therefore, for the 24 population-2 registers where `(relative_addr >> 2) < 32` (since `d->reg[]` is `uint32_t`, `D:53`), the bit **DOES latch** in `d->reg[...]`[cite: 1]! It does NOT "never latch (always-announce)" for those 24 registers[cite: 1].
  2. Furthermore, in `p2 rd census` (`R:26`), each of the 26 population-2 registers is loaded **once** (`26 ld: all 0`, `R:26`)[cite: 1]. Because `odata = d->reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)];` is preloaded at `D:78-79` **before** the switch statement (`D:81`) and **before** `d->reg[...] |= ...` at `D:193`, the first read of `d->reg[relative_addr >> 2]` returns the preloaded `odata` (`0`), and only modifies `d->reg[relative_addr >> 2]` afterwards[cite: 1]! Thus `p2 rd census` reads `0` for all 26 registers and **stays green** (unless `d->reg[0]` is used, which is offset `0`, not even a population-2 register, so `p2 rd census` STILL reads `0` for all 26 population-2 registers and **stays green**!)[cite: 1].

FLAG: `F:34` / `D:220` / `R:62, 64, 69` -- F claims `default:` refusal conditioned on `idata` ("serve zero stores and all reads, or serve non-zero stores") is killed by "`p1 fault lo` (idata is 0 on reads) or `p1 fault wr` + `p1 widths` (0xa5a5a5a5 and the NARROW stores are non-zero)"[cite: 1].
* **One-line diff:** `- return 0;` / `+ if (!idata) return 0; break;` (at `D:220`, serving non-zero stores while faulting when `idata == 0`)[cite: 1].
* **Row staying green:** Whether `p1 fault wr` (`R:64`) goes red or stays green when `default:` serves non-zero stores (`if (!idata) return 0; break;`) is **UNSETTLED** from R and F because `R:64` ("`st at 0x90: PC 0x18, DMA0, DMT0 (the word-store form)`") never states what value is stored by `p1 fault wr` (unlike `R:27` and `R:36`, which state `0xa5a5a5a5` for `p2 wr census` and `p2 ann wr`)[cite: 1]. If `p1 fault wr` stores `0`, it stays green under `if (!idata) return 0; break;`[cite: 1]. The file that would settle this is `vme_default_probe.py`[cite: 1].

